course: dictionary
lesson: 1237
title: Slang and casual words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Slang is informal, everyday language that friends and families use when they talk casually. «The rain was a bummer.» The clue is that the rain spoiled something, so a bummer must be a disappointing thing.
explain: Slang often sounds friendly and relaxed. «What a bummer!» is said when something goes wrong, like saying «What a shame!» or «That's annoying!» You can swap those phrases in to check your meaning.
explain: Slang can change from place to place. Bummer is used most in the USA, Australia and Canada. In Britain, bum is slang for bottom, so some older people think bummer sounds a bit cheeky. Think about who you are talking to before you use slang.
fact: A bummer is a disappointing or annoying thing or situation.
fact: «What a bummer!» means «What a shame!» or «That's annoying!»
fact: Slang is informal language, so it is best used with friends and family, not in formal writing.
fact: Bummer is slang, and it is mostly used in the USA, Australia and Canada.
fact: In Britain, bum is slang for bottom, so some older people think bummer sounds cheeky.
exercise: «The rain was a bummer.» What does bummer mean here? | A happy surprise | *A disappointing or annoying thing | A large, dark cloud | A type of sports game
exercise: Ava's team lost the match and she says, «What a bummer!» What does she mean? | Hooray, we won! | Please pass the salt | *What a shame! | Let's go to the park
exercise: Why might some older British people think bummer sounds cheeky? | *Because bum is slang for bottom in Britain | Because it is a long, formal word | Because it comes from a famous song | Because it means a big party
test: Tom says, «Bummer!» when his ice cream falls on the floor. What is he saying? | Please help me | Hooray! | I am very tired | *That's annoying!
test: Which word is closest in meaning to bummer? | Surprise | Holiday | Treat | *Disappointment
exam: «It was a real bummer that the trip was cancelled.» What does real bummer mean? | A fun adventure | *A disappointing shame | A quiet walk | A kind present
